WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:04.460
SCOTT ELLIS: My
name is Scott Ellis.

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:06.420
I'm a product manager
here at Google

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:10.350
working on privacy,
security-related technology.

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:13.020
And we're going to talk a
little bit about that today.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:18.690
And just to start, did anybody
here attend today's keynote

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:20.562
this morning?

00:00:20.562 --> 00:00:22.020
So we're going to
talk a little bit

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:24.480
about some of the stuff that
was announced there today.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.919
And this is a picture I took
from the back of the room.

00:00:26.919 --> 00:00:28.710
We talked a little bit
about sensitive data

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:31.860
and different tools that
we have to handle it,

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.700
to manage it and
process that data.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.700
And so we'll talk a little
bit about that today,

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:40.020
but first what I
want to cover here

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:46.305
is really talking about data and
collaboration in the modern era

00:00:46.305 --> 00:00:49.066
and what we're doing today,
some of the trends we're seeing,

00:00:49.066 --> 00:00:50.940
and specifically talk
about some of the tools

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:54.360
that we have on our
Cloud platform in G Suite

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.150
and on our production
GCP platform,

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:59.640
and then give a little bit of
background on sensitive data,

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.470
on personally
identifiable information.

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:04.200
And then the heart
of the talk is really

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.880
to welcome some of our friends
here from the University

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.463
of Chicago to talk
a little bit deeper

00:01:07.463 --> 00:01:10.555
about data science,
de-identification, and some

00:01:10.555 --> 00:01:12.930
of the challenges they face
and some of the opportunities

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.640
that they see.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:16.630
So just a few trends.

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:19.390
When we look at
data now, these are

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:22.267
things I picked out that I
think are really kind of driving

00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:23.350
a lot of what we're doing.

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:26.380
So first, we have a lot
more real-time interaction

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:29.320
with our users, with
customers, and with devices.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:32.912
So this is, again, data
coming in, streaming in,

00:01:32.912 --> 00:01:34.870
a lot of back-and-forth
interaction with users.

00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:36.820
It's very fast,
very data driven.

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:40.040
And this is creating a lot
of larger, richer data sets.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.220
And I use that word
"richer" because we all

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:44.020
see these large data
sets and we think

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:47.390
that we can extract out
information from them,

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:51.790
like just valuable information,
learn more about how

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:54.700
our customers or
users are interacting,

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:57.190
predict things that they
might do, and, in some cases,

00:01:57.190 --> 00:02:00.880
even cope with
life-saving predictions

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.660
and life-saving things that
we can learn from that.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:05.684
And as part of that,
as it's real-time

00:02:05.684 --> 00:02:07.600
and we're getting larger
and larger data sets,

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:09.349
we also want to make
faster decisions.

00:02:09.349 --> 00:02:11.140
So we want to be able
to look at this data,

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:12.850
and we want to act
on it very quickly.

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:14.200
We don't want to take
months to figure out

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:16.783
what we should have done, if we
need to interact with somebody

00:02:16.783 --> 00:02:18.050
in seconds or minutes.

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:20.650
So we need to be both
dealing with larger data sets

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:22.559
and making faster decisions.

00:02:22.559 --> 00:02:24.100
And when we talk
about collaboration,

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:26.470
that just means we need
streamlined collaboration.

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:28.390
We need to be able to
talk to each other back

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:30.845
and forth in real time
or near real time.

00:02:30.845 --> 00:02:32.470
We need to be able
to just add somebody

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:36.100
in to a document or
share something with them

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:39.220
and have it just be a
seamless experience.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:41.470
And, again, some of that
data that we're talking about

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:42.220
is sensitive.

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:44.410
So it's not all just bits.

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:47.080
Some of this stuff is very
sensitive information,

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:49.630
specifically sensitive
information about people

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:51.860
that we do need to protect.

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:53.960
So securing this
data is crucial.

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.680
We spend a lot of time looking
across the board of how

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:59.230
we secure data, things like
two factor authentication,

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:01.120
setting the right
access control,

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.550
audit logging for when
people go in and access data,

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:04.630
reviewing those audits--

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:07.090
so generating logs
and auditing them--

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:09.190
encrypting data, and a
whole bunch of other things

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:10.390
that we do.

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:13.390
And in fact, we spend a lot
of time, a lot of effort

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:16.332
in securing data
to mitigate risk.

00:03:16.332 --> 00:03:18.040
But sometimes at some
point, you actually

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:19.539
want to do something
with your data.

00:03:19.539 --> 00:03:21.377
You want to open it up.

00:03:21.377 --> 00:03:23.460
In the last talk, if any
of you saw that with Joe,

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:25.760
that you'd want to use a key.

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:27.220
You might actually
open the vault

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:29.303
and let people in and do
something with your data.

00:03:29.303 --> 00:03:31.010
That's kind of the idea here.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:33.790
And once you do that, the
data itself can be a risk.

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:35.710
So once you do
something, you need

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:37.660
to be aware of who
you're sharing it with,

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:40.720
what you're doing,
and, in some cases,

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:45.680
take action to reduce that
risk in the actual data.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.310
So let's go through an
example with Sheets.

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:50.435
So Google Sheets is a very--

00:03:50.435 --> 00:03:53.160
it's a spreadsheet
app on G Suite that

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:54.700
has built in a lot
of-- it was built

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:56.740
from the ground up with a
lot of collaboration in mind.

00:03:56.740 --> 00:03:57.823
So here's just an example.

00:03:57.823 --> 00:03:58.870
I've got a sheet here.

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:00.100
You can see a column.

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:02.510
We're talking about
customer satisfaction.

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:05.200
One of these customers
has low satisfaction.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:07.090
And I'm looking here,
and I want to know why.

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:10.750
So I want to ping in Janet,
who is a vendor that we work

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:12.315
with from another
company, and ask

00:04:12.315 --> 00:04:13.690
them to go in and
figure out, why

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:15.486
is this customer not satisfied?

00:04:15.486 --> 00:04:17.110
Why is their customer
satisfaction low?

00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:19.120
Maybe they want to contact
them and figure this out.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:20.390
So I just simply go in here.

00:04:20.390 --> 00:04:23.560
I plus them in, put a plus
sign, put their name, ask them

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:25.330
a question, click Go.

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:26.590
Very simple, very easy.

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:28.270
This solves that
streamlined case.

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:30.820
It's very easy to get
in here and do this.

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:32.260
But let's zoom out a little bit.

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:33.759
So we have fast,
easy collaboration,

00:04:33.759 --> 00:04:34.650
but let's zoom out.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:36.400
What we didn't notice
is that in column F,

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:39.150
we had an open-note comment
field that was stuck in here.

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:41.230
And in one of these
comments, somebody

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:43.430
decided to write some
sensitive information.

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:45.250
So this was not my
intent to share this.

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:47.458
I was trying to focus on
this other column-- in fact,

00:04:47.458 --> 00:04:49.150
another row.

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:50.890
But as soon as I
share this with Janet,

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:53.014
she's going to see
the whole spreadsheet.

00:04:53.014 --> 00:04:54.430
So how would we
mitigate something

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:57.219
like this in a corporate app?

00:04:57.219 --> 00:04:59.260
Well, I'll go through an
example of what we have.

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:01.480
So in G Suite, we offer
something called Data Loss

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:04.240
Prevention where you can
set very simple policies

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:05.170
around this.

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:08.494
And this policy is really geared
around three main things--

00:05:08.494 --> 00:05:09.910
triggers, conditions,
and actions.

00:05:09.910 --> 00:05:12.820
So in this case, we look at
something like a trigger.

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:16.090
I'm going to share
this data with somebody

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:17.740
outside of my domain.

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:20.440
The condition is I want to look
for some sensitive information.

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:22.420
In this case, we're going
to look for social security

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:24.503
numbers, driver's license
numbers, and credit card

00:05:24.503 --> 00:05:25.620
numbers.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:27.230
And then I want to
take some action.

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:28.646
So in this case,
I'd probably want

00:05:28.646 --> 00:05:31.120
to block sharing, maybe notify
an admin, things like that.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:34.330
So if we go back to that, and
as soon as I click Comment here,

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:37.350
what's actually happening
in the background is Sheets

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:38.890
will look at this
and say, I need

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:40.995
to go add Janet to this doc.

00:05:40.995 --> 00:05:42.370
She needs permissions
to view it.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:44.920
I need to send her an email
to go click and view it.

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:47.560
But with DLP, we
will first check

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:50.050
to see if there's an issue
with that kind of action

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:54.100
and provide some sort of warning
or block the actual sharing.

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:58.330
In this case, document contains
a social security number,

00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:00.670
and so we can't
share it externally.

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:02.130
And that's pretty
straightforward.

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:05.490
DLP helps organizations
manage their data,

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:08.760
and it can help meet legal,
regulatory, compliance,

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:09.760
contractual obligations.

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:13.210
And it's a pretty standard
operating feature.

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:15.460
But what about production
and development environment?

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:18.510
So this was an issue that
at Google, we face a lot.

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:19.900
We use G Suite.

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:20.800
We love it.

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:22.630
But we have a huge
production environment,

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:24.520
and we seamlessly go
back and forth from one

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:25.877
to the other in a lot of cases.

00:06:25.877 --> 00:06:27.710
And so for us, we were
very concerned about,

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:29.470
what about when you're
developing an app?

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:31.810
What about when you're
dealing with large-scale data

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:34.130
that we call kind of
like production data,

00:06:34.130 --> 00:06:37.300
data that's coming
out of our systems,

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:42.196
out of our applications, out
of our day-to-day business?

00:06:42.196 --> 00:06:44.320
And so this morning, we
talked about a new offering

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:46.582
that we have called the
Data Loss Prevention API.

00:06:46.582 --> 00:06:48.040
What this really
is is we're taking

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:49.960
a lot of that
functionality that you

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:54.340
get to in G Suite going through
a policy and a rules engine

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:56.770
and providing that
capability as an API

00:06:56.770 --> 00:06:59.609
directly into the
Cloud platform.

00:06:59.609 --> 00:07:01.150
And the idea here
is that you can now

00:07:01.150 --> 00:07:04.030
build this into your own apps.

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:06.730
You can take the
same kind of logic

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:10.330
that we built into Gmail
and Drive and Sheets

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:12.700
and build it into your own
applications or workflows.

00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:16.440
And I'll show a
few examples here.

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:20.040
So, again, it's the
same engine that we've

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:23.700
built Gmail and Drive on
and giving you capabilities

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:27.510
like to be able to classify your
data, redact sensitive data,

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:29.130
efficiently manage
sensitive data.

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:32.624
So, again, you may just not
even know what you have.

00:07:32.624 --> 00:07:34.290
You have a lot of
information coming in.

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:35.998
You've got different
assets, and you just

00:07:35.998 --> 00:07:38.230
want to classify them and
manage them appropriately.

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:40.680
And one of the things that
we think this really does

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:42.990
is unlocks some
more of the cloud.

00:07:42.990 --> 00:07:46.110
There's things that
you're not doing today

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:47.970
that you could be doing.

00:07:47.970 --> 00:07:51.630
And by having a little bit
more insight on your data,

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:54.210
we think that it can open
up more of the cloud.

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:55.380
So let's go to an example.

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:57.505
So we showed something in
the keynote this morning.

00:07:57.505 --> 00:07:59.857
We showed a live demo app
of a customer support chat.

00:07:59.857 --> 00:08:02.190
So I'm going to kind of walk
through that use case here.

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:04.990
So imagine you have this
customer support app.

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:07.410
And in there, you have
a chat transcript.

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:08.280
And you chat.

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:10.412
In this case, you've had
a customer interaction.

00:08:10.412 --> 00:08:12.870
And at the very end, maybe you
want to email a copy of that

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:14.130
to the customer.

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:16.710
So you have a simple
action in your app.

00:08:16.710 --> 00:08:18.285
You click Email
Copy to Customer.

00:08:18.285 --> 00:08:20.160
But in this case, we've
detected that there's

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:21.784
sensitive information
in there, and you

00:08:21.784 --> 00:08:23.640
may want to prompt
the user again

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:26.070
in a custom built app
the same kind of way

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:27.594
that we prompted
someone in Sheets.

00:08:27.594 --> 00:08:29.010
So in this case,
we've warned them

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:30.810
that there is a credit card
number, a social security

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:32.142
number, and a phone number.

00:08:32.142 --> 00:08:34.350
And so maybe you don't want
email this to a customer.

00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:37.200
That doesn't show a
very good best practice.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:39.659
So your option could
be just don't do it,

00:08:39.659 --> 00:08:41.889
or maybe you want to go in
and redact that stuff out.

00:08:41.889 --> 00:08:44.430
So the customer still gets the
meaning behind the transcript,

00:08:44.430 --> 00:08:47.013
but you're not revealing their
credit card number to their AOL

00:08:47.013 --> 00:08:50.380
account or wherever it's going.

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:52.257
And another example--
but now let's

00:08:52.257 --> 00:08:54.340
you have that chat transcript,
and you've probably

00:08:54.340 --> 00:08:55.900
got millions of these.

00:08:55.900 --> 00:08:57.670
And so you take that
one chat transcript.

00:08:57.670 --> 00:08:59.740
You export all of them.

00:08:59.740 --> 00:09:01.390
And you want to do
something with it.

00:09:01.390 --> 00:09:04.060
You want to maybe load it up
to bigger analytics, machine

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:06.045
learning, natural
language processing.

00:09:06.045 --> 00:09:08.170
And you kind of want to
analyze what kind of trends

00:09:08.170 --> 00:09:11.950
do I see in my free text
customer support chat.

00:09:11.950 --> 00:09:15.170
So to do that, first you
might want to redact it.

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:17.020
So again, similar
concept-- go in,

00:09:17.020 --> 00:09:18.880
strip out everything
that's in there,

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:22.440
and then send it into something
like analytics or machine

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:23.012
learning.

00:09:23.012 --> 00:09:25.220
So, again, this is just a
safer way to kind of unlock

00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:26.280
more usage in the cloud.

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:31.600
And so the idea
here again is you've

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:33.294
got your raw data
sitting somewhere

00:09:33.294 --> 00:09:34.460
in a production environment.

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:35.992
Maybe it's just sitting on GCS.

00:09:35.992 --> 00:09:37.450
Maybe it's loaded
up into BigQuery.

00:09:37.450 --> 00:09:40.056
Maybe it's just hidden in some
app that you have access to

00:09:40.056 --> 00:09:41.680
and you need to
extract it out, and you

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:45.320
want to get it over here, in
to do higher level analytics,

00:09:45.320 --> 00:09:47.470
machine learning, maybe
just app development.

00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:49.540
So think about wanting
to have test data.

00:09:49.540 --> 00:09:51.340
You want to run
through the UI and see

00:09:51.340 --> 00:09:53.594
what it would look like.

00:09:53.594 --> 00:09:55.510
You maybe have debug
logs that are coming out,

00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:56.980
and you want to analyze
those in a development

00:09:56.980 --> 00:09:58.270
environment, things like that.

00:09:58.270 --> 00:10:01.840
Or you want to improve
how you share this data.

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:03.580
So you're already
doing secure sharing

00:10:03.580 --> 00:10:05.200
with data where you
lock down the ACLs

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:06.890
and make sure the right
people have access.

00:10:06.890 --> 00:10:08.830
But again, maybe they don't
need to see everything.

00:10:08.830 --> 00:10:10.410
Maybe the people
doing analysis only

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:14.080
need to see the bulk content
but not the sensitive bits.

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:16.270
And that's where, again,
using an API like ours,

00:10:16.270 --> 00:10:18.590
you'd look for the
sensitive data,

00:10:18.590 --> 00:10:23.580
go in, redact it, and then again
enable those same use cases.

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:24.580
So try it out.

00:10:24.580 --> 00:10:29.020
So you can go to this site to
find out more about this API.

00:10:29.020 --> 00:10:31.360
We also have a demo
on it that you can

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:33.430
go in and start typing on here.

00:10:33.430 --> 00:10:35.730
And just type stuff in,
play around with it,

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:36.970
see what it detects.

00:10:36.970 --> 00:10:39.460
As you type, you'll see things
filter in at the bottom.

00:10:39.460 --> 00:10:41.981
In this case, I've just
given a couple sample things.

00:10:41.981 --> 00:10:43.480
And you see at the
bottom, it starts

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:45.391
to come out with, hey,
here's what we found.

00:10:45.391 --> 00:10:46.390
We're kind of confident.

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:49.339
We have a certain likelihood
of confidence level here.

00:10:49.339 --> 00:10:51.880
And you start to get an idea of
what you might see in the API

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:55.420
as well, things like offsets,
where this data's found,

00:10:55.420 --> 00:10:57.350
things like that.

00:10:57.350 --> 00:11:01.318
And I'm going to go through and
show you an actual demo here.

00:11:01.318 --> 00:11:03.230
If we just get out of
here-- and this is just

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:08.000
a very, very simple demo we
built, a demo on App Engine

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:10.310
going in and calling this API.

00:11:10.310 --> 00:11:14.510
So in this case, what we've done
is built a simple feedback web

00:11:14.510 --> 00:11:15.330
page.

00:11:15.330 --> 00:11:19.490
So here, a customer could
go in, type something.

00:11:19.490 --> 00:11:21.050
I'm going to go ahead--

00:11:21.050 --> 00:11:24.130
and let's see here--

00:11:24.130 --> 00:11:29.580
and just type in, please
[INAUDIBLE] my credit card.

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:31.120
And this is a valid card.

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:33.470
It's a published test card.

00:11:33.470 --> 00:11:37.069
And it's important that it's
valid for a couple of reasons

00:11:37.069 --> 00:11:37.610
I'll go over.

00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:39.200
So I'm going to submit that.

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:40.900
Thank you for submitting it.

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:43.740
And then we're going to go
into an admin view here.

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:46.220
And we're just going
to see an admin view.

00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:48.800
So this might be something--
again, a customer went in,

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:49.980
submitted something
maybe they shouldn't

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:51.330
have put in a feedback form.

00:11:51.330 --> 00:11:53.214
So your default
admin view would be,

00:11:53.214 --> 00:11:55.130
let's just show the
information but redact out

00:11:55.130 --> 00:11:56.720
the sensitive information here.

00:11:56.720 --> 00:11:59.240
And so what we've built
here is two simple views

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:00.590
calling in the API.

00:12:00.590 --> 00:12:04.320
In this case, we decided to
store the actual raw data

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:06.786
but not display it to
the user by default. So

00:12:06.786 --> 00:12:08.660
what we see here is just
a bunch of asterisks

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:11.270
replaced anytime we saw a PII.

00:12:11.270 --> 00:12:14.210
Now, if a user, if an
admin needs to see that,

00:12:14.210 --> 00:12:16.580
that can be a role-based choice.

00:12:16.580 --> 00:12:18.805
We have a button here
that says, show PII, where

00:12:18.805 --> 00:12:20.180
you can click it
and you'll start

00:12:20.180 --> 00:12:23.120
to see the actual underlying
PII that's in there.

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:26.195
And again, that's just what
we decided to do in this app.

00:12:26.195 --> 00:12:28.820
You might want to generate extra
audit logging for somebody who

00:12:28.820 --> 00:12:32.240
does reveal that or just
restrict it completely

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:35.280
to certain people that can
handle that level of detail.

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:38.367
But just a very simple app
to show the point here.

00:12:38.367 --> 00:12:38.950
And let's see.

00:12:38.950 --> 00:12:39.820
Let's get back.

00:12:39.820 --> 00:12:43.010
And let's just walk
through what we did.

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:46.740
So, again, we took something,
a very simple feedback order

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:47.240
form.

00:12:47.240 --> 00:12:49.357
We sent information in.

00:12:49.357 --> 00:12:51.440
We showed how you can
basically build a view where

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:54.530
that information is
redacted live shown

00:12:54.530 --> 00:12:56.960
to the user with
asterisks in this case,

00:12:56.960 --> 00:12:59.819
and give them the ability to
click and show the actual PII.

00:12:59.819 --> 00:13:02.360
And now I'm going to show you
a little bit of what that looks

00:13:02.360 --> 00:13:04.770
like using a REST API call.

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:09.010
So in the back end, this is what
a simple call would look like.

00:13:09.010 --> 00:13:10.477
And it's in a JSON format.

00:13:10.477 --> 00:13:12.560
So for developers, you'll
be able understand this.

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:14.101
But even if you
can't, you can almost

00:13:14.101 --> 00:13:16.970
read this like just
human text, right?

00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:18.230
I have an item here.

00:13:18.230 --> 00:13:19.136
It's got a value.

00:13:19.136 --> 00:13:21.260
And you can see this might
be what a customer might

00:13:21.260 --> 00:13:22.420
send in his feedback.

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:24.980
"My order did not go through."

00:13:24.980 --> 00:13:27.810
And then we want to look
for certain things here.

00:13:27.810 --> 00:13:30.290
So this is similar to that
policy we saw out in Sheets.

00:13:30.290 --> 00:13:32.450
I want to look for credit
cards, social security

00:13:32.450 --> 00:13:34.380
cards, and email addresses.

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:37.650
Click Send, and you'll
get a response like this.

00:13:37.650 --> 00:13:39.650
So in this case, we
looked for three things,

00:13:39.650 --> 00:13:41.036
but we only found one of them.

00:13:41.036 --> 00:13:42.410
And we give a lot
of detail here.

00:13:42.410 --> 00:13:46.250
So we've said include the quote,
show what kind of likelihood

00:13:46.250 --> 00:13:48.680
we have, and tell me
where you found it.

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:50.619
In a string of text,
it's a simple offset.

00:13:50.619 --> 00:13:52.160
So that's how simple
it is, just even

00:13:52.160 --> 00:13:56.330
using RESTful API calls just
to send data in, send data out.

00:13:56.330 --> 00:13:58.250
We also have options
to do redaction.

00:13:58.250 --> 00:13:59.940
We also support images.

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:02.330
You can do RESTful
calls just based--

00:14:02.330 --> 00:14:05.232
kind of encoding the base-64,
the image, and send it in.

00:14:05.232 --> 00:14:05.940
It's that simple.

00:14:08.790 --> 00:14:11.910
And so how this might be
used, so we've kind of now

00:14:11.910 --> 00:14:13.890
zoomed out on this
app that we built.

00:14:13.890 --> 00:14:17.330
And we said, OK, so we have
an app built on App Engine.

00:14:17.330 --> 00:14:20.280
Just maybe some front
end that we have here.

00:14:20.280 --> 00:14:21.810
And this is the
feedback part of it.

00:14:21.810 --> 00:14:24.450
So what we showed today was
basically taking this API

00:14:24.450 --> 00:14:26.340
and building it in at
the app level where

00:14:26.340 --> 00:14:29.040
you can do classification and
redaction in line, in the app,

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:31.350
make it a functional
part of your application.

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:33.300
But you could also,
in the back end,

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:36.187
do it when you're
working with your data.

00:14:36.187 --> 00:14:37.770
In this case, we
looked at building it

00:14:37.770 --> 00:14:40.680
into a Cloud Dataflow pipeline
before it gets into BigQuery.

00:14:40.680 --> 00:14:43.260
We wanted to go in
and batch in massive--

00:14:43.260 --> 00:14:47.400
like remove the PII
before we hit BigQuery.

00:14:47.400 --> 00:14:49.650
But you could also
use this as a tool

00:14:49.650 --> 00:14:51.540
to just understand
your data and then

00:14:51.540 --> 00:14:53.100
set IAM rules appropriately.

00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:54.870
So you maybe just
have a dynamic thing

00:14:54.870 --> 00:14:57.709
that's scanning every week,
every month, every day.

00:14:57.709 --> 00:14:59.250
And as soon as you
see something that

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:04.030
is classified as, let's say,
having certain sensitive data,

00:15:04.030 --> 00:15:05.540
you lock it down
with an IAM rule

00:15:05.540 --> 00:15:07.690
where only certain people that
are qualified can see that.

00:15:07.690 --> 00:15:09.690
So there's a lot of ways
that you can weave this

00:15:09.690 --> 00:15:11.692
in into your workflow.

00:15:11.692 --> 00:15:13.150
So now I'm going
to go a little bit

00:15:13.150 --> 00:15:15.400
and give a little
bit of background

00:15:15.400 --> 00:15:17.780
on sensitive data and PII.

00:15:17.780 --> 00:15:21.520
And then we will welcome our
friends up on stage as well.

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:23.020
And so what we've
covered today is

00:15:23.020 --> 00:15:26.290
we've talked a lot about finding
sensitive data, specifically

00:15:26.290 --> 00:15:28.930
identifiable data or what's
often called personally

00:15:28.930 --> 00:15:30.970
identifiable information, PII.

00:15:30.970 --> 00:15:35.110
You'll sometimes see the word
"SPII" for Sensitive Personally

00:15:35.110 --> 00:15:36.190
Identifiable Information.

00:15:36.190 --> 00:15:39.980
You'll see the word "PHI,"
which people in the health world

00:15:39.980 --> 00:15:41.230
know exactly what that means--

00:15:41.230 --> 00:15:42.495
Protected Health Information.

00:15:42.495 --> 00:15:43.870
I've been in a
lot of rooms where

00:15:43.870 --> 00:15:46.180
people think it means
personal health information

00:15:46.180 --> 00:15:48.402
or something like that.

00:15:48.402 --> 00:15:50.110
But really what it is
is it's information

00:15:50.110 --> 00:15:52.120
protected under HIPAA.

00:15:52.120 --> 00:15:53.960
And it breaks into two parts.

00:15:53.960 --> 00:15:56.000
And the last talk
really covered this,

00:15:56.000 --> 00:15:58.030
but it has to be
protected under HIPAA.

00:15:58.030 --> 00:15:59.990
You have to be coming
from a covered entity.

00:15:59.990 --> 00:16:03.260
But it also means that it's
data that is identifiable.

00:16:03.260 --> 00:16:05.680
And so it does have some
overlap with just identifiers

00:16:05.680 --> 00:16:07.610
and personally
identifiable information.

00:16:07.610 --> 00:16:09.670
So some of these are
pretty obvious if you

00:16:09.670 --> 00:16:11.980
look at this-- names, social
security numbers, passport

00:16:11.980 --> 00:16:14.170
numbers, telephone
numbers, various things

00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:17.110
that you can find with our API.

00:16:17.110 --> 00:16:18.700
And it also talks
about some things

00:16:18.700 --> 00:16:20.460
that are trickier to find.

00:16:20.460 --> 00:16:21.790
And we're going to go into
a little bit of background

00:16:21.790 --> 00:16:23.410
about what those are and
other things that you

00:16:23.410 --> 00:16:24.712
can do to kind of handle that.

00:16:24.712 --> 00:16:26.170
But one is highlighted
here in blue

00:16:26.170 --> 00:16:28.600
is persistent static
identifier, something

00:16:28.600 --> 00:16:32.890
that is not well-defined but
maybe identifies a small group

00:16:32.890 --> 00:16:35.620
or a single individual.

00:16:35.620 --> 00:16:38.787
So let's go take a look at
what classification looks like

00:16:38.787 --> 00:16:40.370
or what are the
things that we do when

00:16:40.370 --> 00:16:41.740
we're classifying this data.

00:16:41.740 --> 00:16:44.630
So we'll go with a
very simple example.

00:16:44.630 --> 00:16:47.510
Again, this is my credit card
followed by a string of digits.

00:16:47.510 --> 00:16:51.590
So what we look at here
is a bunch of clues.

00:16:51.590 --> 00:16:53.277
We can look at things
like structure.

00:16:53.277 --> 00:16:54.860
I mean, this looks
like a credit card.

00:16:54.860 --> 00:16:58.230
It has dashes between
every four digits.

00:16:58.230 --> 00:17:00.980
We also have some algorithms
that can go and actually check

00:17:00.980 --> 00:17:01.550
this number.

00:17:01.550 --> 00:17:03.932
And it's called a checksum
in case of credit cards,

00:17:03.932 --> 00:17:05.390
where it looks at
the number itself

00:17:05.390 --> 00:17:07.040
and validates that it's real.

00:17:07.040 --> 00:17:09.380
So if you put just 16
digit numbers with dashes,

00:17:09.380 --> 00:17:11.119
that doesn't mean
it's a credit card.

00:17:11.119 --> 00:17:12.535
In this case, we
can check that it

00:17:12.535 --> 00:17:15.230
is a real credit card or at
least passes the test for one.

00:17:15.230 --> 00:17:17.060
We also can look
at context clues.

00:17:17.060 --> 00:17:19.660
In this case, it's pretty clear
that this is a credit card.

00:17:19.660 --> 00:17:20.990
There's context saying it.

00:17:20.990 --> 00:17:22.099
There's format saying it.

00:17:22.099 --> 00:17:24.440
And there's some
math effectively

00:17:24.440 --> 00:17:27.319
analyzing this and saying it.

00:17:27.319 --> 00:17:28.910
And so what do we do with that?

00:17:28.910 --> 00:17:33.130
So we showed today one way is
to just simply redact it out.

00:17:33.130 --> 00:17:35.065
Other things you
can do is once you

00:17:35.065 --> 00:17:36.940
know where it is, you
can go in and do things

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:38.830
like hashing or tokenization.

00:17:38.830 --> 00:17:41.700
So in this case, we're showing
just sort of a random hash.

00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:45.260
And we purposely made one
that doesn't look very pretty,

00:17:45.260 --> 00:17:46.120
but that's fine.

00:17:46.120 --> 00:17:48.160
It's a hash, the idea
being that every time I

00:17:48.160 --> 00:17:49.810
see a certain
credit card, maybe I

00:17:49.810 --> 00:17:51.220
want to generate the same hash.

00:17:51.220 --> 00:17:54.370
That opens up a lot of
opportunities for trend

00:17:54.370 --> 00:17:55.990
analysis and analytics.

00:17:55.990 --> 00:17:58.490
But you can also do things like
format-preserving encryption

00:17:58.490 --> 00:18:00.510
and format-preserving tokens.

00:18:00.510 --> 00:18:03.010
So in this case, I've generated
something that kind of looks

00:18:03.010 --> 00:18:06.390
and acts like a credit
card, but it isn't.

00:18:06.390 --> 00:18:08.585
It's a token that
represents the credit card.

00:18:08.585 --> 00:18:10.960
This is important if you were
really strict on the format

00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:13.540
that you have, if
you're using it in a UI

00:18:13.540 --> 00:18:15.040
where you want to
display something

00:18:15.040 --> 00:18:16.660
and you want the
end user to see it

00:18:16.660 --> 00:18:19.090
as it would be whereas
displaying the middle one

00:18:19.090 --> 00:18:20.470
would look kind of off.

00:18:20.470 --> 00:18:22.064
It might look like
a bug to them.

00:18:22.064 --> 00:18:23.980
So there's a lot of
techniques for obfuscating

00:18:23.980 --> 00:18:25.045
this kind of data.

00:18:25.045 --> 00:18:28.504
The first step is go find it.

00:18:28.504 --> 00:18:29.920
But there's some
challenges there.

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:32.003
So we're going to talk
about one of the challenges

00:18:32.003 --> 00:18:33.950
here is names.

00:18:33.950 --> 00:18:37.144
So here's a very simple example,
and I'm looking at a case

00:18:37.144 --> 00:18:38.560
where the stuff
in green, you have

00:18:38.560 --> 00:18:41.342
green boxes around the stuff
we found and we classified.

00:18:41.342 --> 00:18:42.550
And we missed something here.

00:18:42.550 --> 00:18:44.740
So we missed the
last name of Zhang.

00:18:44.740 --> 00:18:48.612
So if were to use a
redaction in this,

00:18:48.612 --> 00:18:50.320
very straightforward--
take all the stuff

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:51.752
we found and put Xs there.

00:18:51.752 --> 00:18:53.710
That works, except for
what we've actually done

00:18:53.710 --> 00:18:56.632
is very obviously highlighted
the thing we missed.

00:18:56.632 --> 00:18:58.090
So anyone looking
at this that sees

00:18:58.090 --> 00:19:00.666
Xs anytime they see a
name knows we found it,

00:19:00.666 --> 00:19:01.540
knows we redacted it.

00:19:01.540 --> 00:19:03.282
So when you see a
random name in there

00:19:03.282 --> 00:19:05.740
that a human can pick out, they
kind of know you missed it.

00:19:05.740 --> 00:19:08.010
It probably means
it's the real thing.

00:19:08.010 --> 00:19:09.260
So there's other options here.

00:19:09.260 --> 00:19:12.079
So NIST talked a
little bit about this.

00:19:12.079 --> 00:19:13.870
And it's a common thing
of basically hiding

00:19:13.870 --> 00:19:14.950
in plain sight.

00:19:14.950 --> 00:19:18.040
But it's the idea of using
surrogate replacements

00:19:18.040 --> 00:19:20.200
or realistic surrogate
replacements.

00:19:20.200 --> 00:19:23.080
So instead of Xs,
we use other names.

00:19:23.080 --> 00:19:24.710
So you could go in
here, for example.

00:19:24.710 --> 00:19:26.044
Take the same example.

00:19:26.044 --> 00:19:28.210
and now we're going to
replace "Janet" with "Cindy."

00:19:28.210 --> 00:19:29.980
And we're going to replace
"Smith" with "Roberts."

00:19:29.980 --> 00:19:31.240
And we're going to
leave this thing

00:19:31.240 --> 00:19:33.073
we missed because,
again, we didn't catch it

00:19:33.073 --> 00:19:34.130
so we can't change it.

00:19:34.130 --> 00:19:37.270
And now in the final output,
we still missed Zhang,

00:19:37.270 --> 00:19:38.680
but it's harder to find out.

00:19:38.680 --> 00:19:40.270
It's harder to see
that we missed it.

00:19:40.270 --> 00:19:41.200
Again, it's not foolproof.

00:19:41.200 --> 00:19:43.116
There are [? attacks. ?]
You can look at this.

00:19:43.116 --> 00:19:44.620
But it's things
like this that you

00:19:44.620 --> 00:19:46.900
can do to reduce the risk
of somebody looking at this

00:19:46.900 --> 00:19:48.550
and obviously finding
the information

00:19:48.550 --> 00:19:49.990
that you didn't
want them to find.

00:19:49.990 --> 00:19:53.050
So this is an example of
hiding in plain sight.

00:19:53.050 --> 00:19:54.640
And, again, pointing
it out here,

00:19:54.640 --> 00:19:57.319
this is kind of like
tokenizing, what

00:19:57.319 --> 00:19:58.610
we showed with the credit card.

00:19:58.610 --> 00:20:01.930
What we're really doing is
we're using a realistic name

00:20:01.930 --> 00:20:03.220
as a replacement there.

00:20:03.220 --> 00:20:05.230
And we've done it, in
this case, with integrity

00:20:05.230 --> 00:20:08.320
where "Janet" is replaced
with "Cindy" in all cases.

00:20:08.320 --> 00:20:10.240
A very simple example
here, but if did

00:20:10.240 --> 00:20:13.569
want to know that this is the
same name, you can do that.

00:20:13.569 --> 00:20:15.360
And now we'll just look
at one last concept

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:21.500
here that kind of touches
on that persistent static

00:20:21.500 --> 00:20:22.030
identifier.

00:20:22.030 --> 00:20:24.000
And it's the concept
of k-anonymity.

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:26.360
And I'll do a very
rough example of this,

00:20:26.360 --> 00:20:28.020
but imagine you've
stripped out all

00:20:28.020 --> 00:20:30.049
of the sensitive
information that you think

00:20:30.049 --> 00:20:30.840
is in your dataset.

00:20:30.840 --> 00:20:34.530
You stripped down credit cards,
social security cards, maybe

00:20:34.530 --> 00:20:35.037
even names.

00:20:35.037 --> 00:20:36.120
You've got it really down.

00:20:36.120 --> 00:20:37.790
But what you have
is you have a data

00:20:37.790 --> 00:20:39.335
set with some
sensitive information.

00:20:39.335 --> 00:20:41.460
Let's just say it's salary
information or something

00:20:41.460 --> 00:20:44.130
about employees.

00:20:44.130 --> 00:20:46.080
In this case, let's
say it's Googlers.

00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:48.270
And you wanted to do some
analysis on job titles.

00:20:48.270 --> 00:20:50.430
So what you want to do is
keep their job title in,

00:20:50.430 --> 00:20:52.440
look at maybe salaries
and promotions and things

00:20:52.440 --> 00:20:55.080
that could be sensitive,
and do some analysis on it.

00:20:55.080 --> 00:20:57.815
Well, in this case, it seems
pretty straightforward,

00:20:57.815 --> 00:21:01.950
but an obvious problem here is
something like CEO of Google.

00:21:01.950 --> 00:21:04.500
It's not hard for anyone in
this room to guess who that is.

00:21:04.500 --> 00:21:05.830
Today, it would be one person.

00:21:05.830 --> 00:21:07.330
If you looked over
a 10-year period,

00:21:07.330 --> 00:21:10.530
it's still down to three
people pretty much.

00:21:10.530 --> 00:21:11.440
So that's an outlier.

00:21:11.440 --> 00:21:12.814
That's something
that we wouldn't

00:21:12.814 --> 00:21:14.670
have thought of as
a unique identifier,

00:21:14.670 --> 00:21:16.350
but it pretty much is.

00:21:16.350 --> 00:21:18.150
Software engineer, not so much.

00:21:18.150 --> 00:21:20.527
But you can imagine there's
a whole spectrum here.

00:21:20.527 --> 00:21:22.110
So there's a whole
spectrum of what we

00:21:22.110 --> 00:21:24.090
call these high-risk outliers.

00:21:24.090 --> 00:21:26.730
In our case at Google, we have
a lot of different engineering

00:21:26.730 --> 00:21:27.357
titles.

00:21:27.357 --> 00:21:28.440
There's software engineer.

00:21:28.440 --> 00:21:30.000
There's senior
software engineer.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:31.740
There's distinguished engineer.

00:21:31.740 --> 00:21:33.480
There's things like
Google Fellows.

00:21:33.480 --> 00:21:36.360
All of these things are
different job titles,

00:21:36.360 --> 00:21:39.270
and some of them are relatively
unique, relatively rare,

00:21:39.270 --> 00:21:41.522
and which fall into a
high-risk outlier group.

00:21:41.522 --> 00:21:43.230
So those are the kind
of things where you

00:21:43.230 --> 00:21:45.000
can do distribution analysis.

00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:47.370
And what k-anonymity does
is looks at how many people

00:21:47.370 --> 00:21:50.080
group into that
particular attribute.

00:21:50.080 --> 00:21:52.140
So in a simple way,
software engineer,

00:21:52.140 --> 00:21:54.240
in this small example,
would have a k of four.

00:21:54.240 --> 00:21:56.910
There's four people in
it just in this sample.

00:21:56.910 --> 00:21:58.507
And CEO of Google
has a k of one.

00:21:58.507 --> 00:22:00.090
So if you were to
think of a threshold

00:22:00.090 --> 00:22:01.620
that you'd want to set--

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:03.240
again, this is a
very small data size,

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:04.698
but you'd want your
threshold maybe

00:22:04.698 --> 00:22:07.254
to be two or three in
this small example.

00:22:07.254 --> 00:22:08.670
But in a larger
dataset, you might

00:22:08.670 --> 00:22:11.363
be looking at setting a
threshold of 10, 20, 100,

00:22:11.363 --> 00:22:13.530
1,000, really depending
on the use case

00:22:13.530 --> 00:22:16.304
and how sensitive
that information is.

00:22:16.304 --> 00:22:18.720
So this is, again, just one
more kind of a little bit more

00:22:18.720 --> 00:22:20.530
advanced concept,
but an important one

00:22:20.530 --> 00:22:24.100
to consider when you're
looking at sensitive data.

00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:26.250
So what we've covered
here, again, is some tools

00:22:26.250 --> 00:22:29.340
that we have to
help you in G Suite,

00:22:29.340 --> 00:22:31.500
and specifically, taking
some of those tools

00:22:31.500 --> 00:22:34.800
and giving the power to you to
weave them into your own apps,

00:22:34.800 --> 00:22:37.560
weave them into your own
workflows, your own pipelines.

00:22:37.560 --> 00:22:42.030
You can find out more
about this again online,

00:22:42.030 --> 00:22:44.910
cloud.google.com/dlp.

00:22:44.910 --> 00:22:48.240
And now what I'd like to do
is welcome some of our friends

00:22:48.240 --> 00:22:51.840
up on stage, Michael and Sam
from University of Chicago,

00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:54.060
to talk a little bit more
about the challenges.

00:22:54.060 --> 00:22:55.870
So I've covered
some of the basics,

00:22:55.870 --> 00:22:57.369
but what we really
want to hear from

00:22:57.369 --> 00:23:01.080
is real data scientists
who face this challenge

00:23:01.080 --> 00:23:03.060
and want to do real
things with data

00:23:03.060 --> 00:23:05.850
and face this issue with
sensitive information and data

00:23:05.850 --> 00:23:06.554
identification.

00:23:06.554 --> 00:23:08.220
And so I'm going to
hand it off to them.

00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:08.460
Great.

00:23:08.460 --> 00:23:09.930
SAMUEL VOLCHENBOUM:
Thanks, Scott.

00:23:09.930 --> 00:23:11.305
MICHAEL HOWELL:
Thank you, Scott.

00:23:11.305 --> 00:23:13.170
So we're going to try
and share a little bit

00:23:13.170 --> 00:23:18.140
about how we look at
this as physicians,

00:23:18.140 --> 00:23:19.950
as folks in the
health care sector,

00:23:19.950 --> 00:23:24.690
and as people who use large data
sets to try and make discovery.

00:23:24.690 --> 00:23:27.300
And we're also going to try
and share some things where

00:23:27.300 --> 00:23:30.270
we feel like there
may be problems

00:23:30.270 --> 00:23:34.800
around de-identification that
we're hopeful will get solved

00:23:34.800 --> 00:23:37.350
in the next few years.

00:23:37.350 --> 00:23:40.480
And so where we're
going to start with is,

00:23:40.480 --> 00:23:43.320
yeah, we're a couple of
doctors who are up here,

00:23:43.320 --> 00:23:47.700
and I suspect several people are
wondering what conference you

00:23:47.700 --> 00:23:48.930
think we're at.

00:23:48.930 --> 00:23:51.900
We actually think that cloud
storage and de-identification

00:23:51.900 --> 00:23:53.910
are important.

00:23:53.910 --> 00:23:56.490
We think they're critical to
improving the pace of discovery

00:23:56.490 --> 00:23:58.615
in health care, and we're
going to try and convince

00:23:58.615 --> 00:24:00.072
you of that.

00:24:00.072 --> 00:24:01.530
We're going to try
and convince you

00:24:01.530 --> 00:24:04.590
that it's important to the
kind of research that we do.

00:24:04.590 --> 00:24:07.020
And at the end of
the next 15 minutes,

00:24:07.020 --> 00:24:09.610
we think you'll agree with that.

00:24:09.610 --> 00:24:12.610
So I'm a doc.

00:24:12.610 --> 00:24:13.780
I'm an ICU doc.

00:24:13.780 --> 00:24:18.720
I have some grants to
study things like this.

00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:20.290
It is important for
me to say that I

00:24:20.290 --> 00:24:22.567
sit on a couple of committees
that provide advice

00:24:22.567 --> 00:24:23.650
to the federal government.

00:24:23.650 --> 00:24:28.360
The things I will talk about
are my opinions, not theirs.

00:24:28.360 --> 00:24:30.790
But I do want you to
know that I'm an ICU doc.

00:24:30.790 --> 00:24:31.810
I'm a critical care doc.

00:24:31.810 --> 00:24:35.470
That influences how
I think about things.

00:24:35.470 --> 00:24:37.660
It's a specialty most
people have never heard of.

00:24:37.660 --> 00:24:40.840
We're the specialty that cares
for you if your risk of dying

00:24:40.840 --> 00:24:44.470
is very high in
the next 24 hours.

00:24:44.470 --> 00:24:47.240
I'm the chief quality officer
of the University of Chicago,

00:24:47.240 --> 00:24:50.560
which is a moderate-sized
health system in the Midwest.

00:24:50.560 --> 00:24:52.650
I work with what,
for health care,

00:24:52.650 --> 00:24:55.570
are reasonably sized data sets.

00:24:55.570 --> 00:25:00.640
And I've experienced both
good care and horrible care

00:25:00.640 --> 00:25:04.240
perpetrated on my
family and feel a need

00:25:04.240 --> 00:25:06.900
to try and make that better.

00:25:06.900 --> 00:25:09.510
SAMUEL VOLCHENBOUM: So
I'm San Volchenboum.

00:25:09.510 --> 00:25:11.330
I'm at the University
of Chicago.

00:25:11.330 --> 00:25:14.070
And I just wanted to point out
a few things about the work

00:25:14.070 --> 00:25:14.650
that I do.

00:25:14.650 --> 00:25:17.100
So I have received and
I do receive funding

00:25:17.100 --> 00:25:19.830
from the government for
some of the work that I do.

00:25:19.830 --> 00:25:22.500
And those grants
are disclosed here.

00:25:22.500 --> 00:25:25.510
I'm also a co-founder
of a company called

00:25:25.510 --> 00:25:28.770
Litmus Health which is
building an analytics platform

00:25:28.770 --> 00:25:30.720
to collect mobile data
for clinical trials.

00:25:30.720 --> 00:25:34.560
So that work has given me
an interesting perspective

00:25:34.560 --> 00:25:37.394
into how commercial
entities have

00:25:37.394 --> 00:25:39.060
to think about privacy
of data, and I'll

00:25:39.060 --> 00:25:42.450
try to bring some of
that to the talk today.

00:25:42.450 --> 00:25:45.300
I have sat on a few
government committees.

00:25:45.300 --> 00:25:46.680
The ones that are
mostly relevant

00:25:46.680 --> 00:25:49.740
are things around building
data standards for things

00:25:49.740 --> 00:25:52.620
like mobile devices
and for how we collect

00:25:52.620 --> 00:25:54.750
data for clinical trials.

00:25:54.750 --> 00:25:57.510
My day job is that I'm trained
as a pediatric oncologist.

00:25:57.510 --> 00:26:00.570
So I take care of kids with
cancer and blood diseases.

00:26:00.570 --> 00:26:03.022
I spend most of my time
running the Center for Research

00:26:03.022 --> 00:26:04.980
Informatics at the
University of Chicago, which

00:26:04.980 --> 00:26:07.710
is a 40-person group
providing informatics support

00:26:07.710 --> 00:26:09.210
to the division.

00:26:09.210 --> 00:26:12.420
My training is mostly in
medicine and molecular biology,

00:26:12.420 --> 00:26:14.280
but I took a detour
to get a degree

00:26:14.280 --> 00:26:16.740
in biomedical informatics.

00:26:16.740 --> 00:26:19.230
And so that's how my three
worlds come together now.

00:26:19.230 --> 00:26:21.270
And what I really try
to do is to figure out

00:26:21.270 --> 00:26:24.064
how we can take data and
how we can democratize it

00:26:24.064 --> 00:26:25.980
so that people can use
it to study and improve

00:26:25.980 --> 00:26:28.027
health outcomes.

00:26:28.027 --> 00:26:30.360
MICHAEL HOWELL: So we're going
to start with a question,

00:26:30.360 --> 00:26:33.100
and we're going to come back
to this a little bit later

00:26:33.100 --> 00:26:33.630
in the talk.

00:26:33.630 --> 00:26:35.200
And the question is just this.

00:26:35.200 --> 00:26:36.783
What is the difference
between a heart

00:26:36.783 --> 00:26:38.650
attack and a cardiac arrest?

00:26:38.650 --> 00:26:41.920
It turns out that actually
most people don't know this.

00:26:41.920 --> 00:26:46.000
95% of people who have a heart
attack in the United States

00:26:46.000 --> 00:26:47.950
today survive.

00:26:47.950 --> 00:26:50.740
And cardiac arrest is the
recognition that you are dead,

00:26:50.740 --> 00:26:54.890
and so it is a somewhat
more serious condition.

00:26:54.890 --> 00:26:56.890
And we're going to come
back to the question of,

00:26:56.890 --> 00:26:58.540
wouldn't you like
to know it's coming

00:26:58.540 --> 00:26:59.782
so that you can prevent it?

00:26:59.782 --> 00:27:01.240
And we're going to
try and show you

00:27:01.240 --> 00:27:03.370
how we've used data
to address that

00:27:03.370 --> 00:27:06.010
at the University of Chicago.

00:27:06.010 --> 00:27:08.440
We do want to point out some
things about health care

00:27:08.440 --> 00:27:12.080
that may be different than
other areas that use data.

00:27:12.080 --> 00:27:14.270
The first is what the
office looks like.

00:27:14.270 --> 00:27:17.620
And so this is the
picture of an ICU

00:27:17.620 --> 00:27:22.090
that Google legal would
allow me to take a look at.

00:27:22.090 --> 00:27:23.890
But if you look at
this here, there

00:27:23.890 --> 00:27:27.880
are one, two, three, four, five,
six, seven, eight, nine, 10,

00:27:27.880 --> 00:27:33.040
11, 12 continuous
infusion IV pumps.

00:27:33.040 --> 00:27:35.980
You can see that this
child is actually

00:27:35.980 --> 00:27:39.790
intubated, on life support,
on mechanical ventilation.

00:27:39.790 --> 00:27:41.830
There are number of other
monitors and things.

00:27:41.830 --> 00:27:43.750
There's a $10,000 bed.

00:27:43.750 --> 00:27:46.750
There's two tubes invading
the thoracic cavity here

00:27:46.750 --> 00:27:48.880
to keep the lung open.

00:27:48.880 --> 00:27:52.710
And oh, yeah, there's a
patient in the middle of that.

00:27:52.710 --> 00:27:55.240
What we don't talk about is
that each of these devices

00:27:55.240 --> 00:27:57.070
generate data.

00:27:57.070 --> 00:28:00.520
The data are not aggregated
in a standard way.

00:28:00.520 --> 00:28:02.110
They're not stored
in a standard way.

00:28:02.110 --> 00:28:04.480
They're not joined
in a standard way.

00:28:04.480 --> 00:28:06.970
And all of this happens
in an environment

00:28:06.970 --> 00:28:11.590
that is chaotic and
difficult to influence, even

00:28:11.590 --> 00:28:14.440
with the world's
fanciest algorithms.

00:28:14.440 --> 00:28:18.160
Our instant messaging queue may
also look different than yours.

00:28:18.160 --> 00:28:20.620
These devices, which many
of you will recognize

00:28:20.620 --> 00:28:23.080
from history books,
are wired directly

00:28:23.080 --> 00:28:24.130
into our adrenal glands.

00:28:24.130 --> 00:28:27.082
And they come across with these
things like, "Hi, Dr. Howell.

00:28:27.082 --> 00:28:28.540
Can you come see
patient so and so?

00:28:28.540 --> 00:28:31.010
Suctioning blood from
the airway and GI tract.

00:28:31.010 --> 00:28:33.350
Now on as much oxygen as
it's possible to give.

00:28:33.350 --> 00:28:34.740
Thanks, Sarah."

00:28:34.740 --> 00:28:37.480
Our residents and trainees are
very polite at the University

00:28:37.480 --> 00:28:38.710
of Chicago.

00:28:38.710 --> 00:28:40.210
Or "Hi, Dr. Howell.

00:28:40.210 --> 00:28:42.880
Just took over care for
this patient in the ICU.

00:28:42.880 --> 00:28:44.940
Significant
respiratory distress.

00:28:44.940 --> 00:28:47.400
Anesthesia coming to
start life support.

00:28:47.400 --> 00:28:50.290
Think she would benefit
from thrombectomy," which

00:28:50.290 --> 00:28:52.750
means having her chest cut
open, her pulmonary artery

00:28:52.750 --> 00:28:55.170
incised, and a clot taken out.

00:28:55.170 --> 00:28:57.250
And so those things are
disruptive to workflow

00:28:57.250 --> 00:28:58.750
when you're trying
to plan your day,

00:28:58.750 --> 00:29:02.290
but they also represent a
piece of absolutely critical

00:29:02.290 --> 00:29:06.804
information that is stored
and analyzed nowhere.

00:29:06.804 --> 00:29:08.470
SAMUEL VOLCHENBOUM:
So one of the things

00:29:08.470 --> 00:29:10.540
that I'm hoping that you'll
be convinced of today

00:29:10.540 --> 00:29:12.790
is that the way we think
about privacy in health care

00:29:12.790 --> 00:29:16.000
is potentially different than
the way people that are not

00:29:16.000 --> 00:29:18.100
in health care think about it.

00:29:18.100 --> 00:29:19.780
The things that we
ask of our patients

00:29:19.780 --> 00:29:21.760
are very, very personal.

00:29:21.760 --> 00:29:26.440
And Michael takes care
of patients in the ICU.

00:29:26.440 --> 00:29:28.750
I take care of
families with children

00:29:28.750 --> 00:29:31.990
with cancer, many of
whom go to the ICU.

00:29:31.990 --> 00:29:33.820
And the things we
ask of our patients

00:29:33.820 --> 00:29:36.610
are things we do at the
worst times in their life.

00:29:36.610 --> 00:29:40.090
And you can imagine that we're
asking them to discuss things

00:29:40.090 --> 00:29:43.630
about death, things
about struggles

00:29:43.630 --> 00:29:44.772
they're having at home.

00:29:44.772 --> 00:29:47.230
Think about the things that
you'll discuss with your doctor

00:29:47.230 --> 00:29:50.860
without thinking twice,
things about pregnancy,

00:29:50.860 --> 00:29:53.170
about psychiatric illness.

00:29:53.170 --> 00:29:54.791
It's incredible
the kinds of things

00:29:54.791 --> 00:29:56.540
we'll share with our
health care provider.

00:29:56.540 --> 00:29:58.390
And we want to do that
with the assumption

00:29:58.390 --> 00:30:00.670
that those data are
going to remain private,

00:30:00.670 --> 00:30:03.790
and that if our health
care data are shared,

00:30:03.790 --> 00:30:07.280
they're done so in a way
that respects that privacy.

00:30:07.280 --> 00:30:10.010
I'm going to show a
couple examples of people

00:30:10.010 --> 00:30:12.190
that had really good
intentions about sharing data,

00:30:12.190 --> 00:30:15.680
but that when they
published their data,

00:30:15.680 --> 00:30:17.720
they did so in a way
that actually exposed

00:30:17.720 --> 00:30:21.830
patients' medical records
and invaded their privacy.

00:30:21.830 --> 00:30:24.410
This is a landmark paper
from about 15 years

00:30:24.410 --> 00:30:27.960
ago from Latanya Sweeney that
actually focuses on k-anonymity

00:30:27.960 --> 00:30:29.750
like Scott was referring to.

00:30:29.750 --> 00:30:31.490
And here are two data
sets that they were

00:30:31.490 --> 00:30:32.615
able to get their hands on.

00:30:36.790 --> 00:30:39.310
The data set on the
left is medical data

00:30:39.310 --> 00:30:41.020
that was released
with good intentions.

00:30:41.020 --> 00:30:42.520
Whoever released
this data set felt

00:30:42.520 --> 00:30:46.660
that this was going to
be data that was private,

00:30:46.660 --> 00:30:50.770
that wasn't going to give
up anybody's privacy.

00:30:50.770 --> 00:30:53.650
They had things like their
diagnosis, procedures

00:30:53.650 --> 00:30:56.680
they had, and then three
seemingly innocuous items--

00:30:56.680 --> 00:30:59.360
their zip code, their
birthday, and their gender.

00:30:59.360 --> 00:31:02.080
On the right is a voter
registration list which they

00:31:02.080 --> 00:31:04.000
requested which was
publicly available that

00:31:04.000 --> 00:31:07.540
had a bunch of things, seemingly
innocuous-- name, address,

00:31:07.540 --> 00:31:09.100
their party
affiliation, but also

00:31:09.100 --> 00:31:11.855
had zip code, birthday, and sex.

00:31:11.855 --> 00:31:13.480
And I don't think
it's really difficult

00:31:13.480 --> 00:31:15.970
to see that if you were to
combine those two data sets,

00:31:15.970 --> 00:31:17.595
you could match on those three.

00:31:17.595 --> 00:31:18.970
And what they were
able to do was

00:31:18.970 --> 00:31:21.910
to identify many of the
patients in this set,

00:31:21.910 --> 00:31:23.920
including the governor
of Massachusetts

00:31:23.920 --> 00:31:27.130
at the time whose data was here.

00:31:27.130 --> 00:31:28.780
Again, these folks
had good intentions

00:31:28.780 --> 00:31:29.710
that published these data.

00:31:29.710 --> 00:31:31.751
But when you looked into
the sets they published,

00:31:31.751 --> 00:31:36.070
they were able to expose
some of the privacy.

00:31:36.070 --> 00:31:38.410
This, I think, is a
fascinating example.

00:31:38.410 --> 00:31:40.180
So this was a paper
that was published

00:31:40.180 --> 00:31:41.950
showing an influenza outbreak.

00:31:41.950 --> 00:31:43.870
This is Boston, and
each of the red dots

00:31:43.870 --> 00:31:46.634
is a site of an influenza
outbreak in Boston.

00:31:46.634 --> 00:31:48.550
And what the authors
hoped to do in this study

00:31:48.550 --> 00:31:51.520
was be able to understand
where the sites of influenza

00:31:51.520 --> 00:31:54.020
were so they could
track the outbreak.

00:31:54.020 --> 00:31:55.780
And so they published
this in a paper.

00:31:55.780 --> 00:32:00.529
And this research group at
Harvard took this set of data.

00:32:00.529 --> 00:32:02.320
And when I teach this,
I say, how would you

00:32:02.320 --> 00:32:03.730
solve this problem?

00:32:03.730 --> 00:32:07.000
And almost always somebody
raises their hand and says,

00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:09.100
I would just put a
Google map on top of it.

00:32:09.100 --> 00:32:11.225
And of course, that's what
this research group did.

00:32:11.225 --> 00:32:12.760
They overlaid a
Google map on top.

00:32:12.760 --> 00:32:15.970
And very quickly, they
were able to identify

00:32:15.970 --> 00:32:19.510
80% of the dots on the
map to the exact address.

00:32:19.510 --> 00:32:21.700
And when they
relaxed the radius,

00:32:21.700 --> 00:32:25.390
they were able to identify 100%
of the people in the study.

00:32:25.390 --> 00:32:27.580
So an incredible
invasion of privacy,

00:32:27.580 --> 00:32:29.380
completely
unintentioned, seemingly

00:32:29.380 --> 00:32:31.450
innocuous to put dots on a map.

00:32:31.450 --> 00:32:33.207
And when I teach
about this, we talk

00:32:33.207 --> 00:32:35.290
about things like, how
would you obscure the data?

00:32:35.290 --> 00:32:37.581
And students will say, well,
you would shift everything

00:32:37.581 --> 00:32:39.340
by 10 feet in some
random direction,

00:32:39.340 --> 00:32:40.630
and that would do it.

00:32:40.630 --> 00:32:43.420
And yes, that would do it
in the middle of Boston.

00:32:43.420 --> 00:32:45.739
But if you shift everybody
10 feet in South Dakota,

00:32:45.739 --> 00:32:48.280
you're going to move somebody
from their house to their barn,

00:32:48.280 --> 00:32:53.230
and then you won't protect
anybody's anonymity--

00:32:53.230 --> 00:32:54.857
no offense to South Dakota.

00:32:54.857 --> 00:32:56.440
But that's the point
here is that it's

00:32:56.440 --> 00:32:57.720
hard to do these problems.

00:32:57.720 --> 00:33:00.340
You have to know the
population you're studying.

00:33:00.340 --> 00:33:03.640
As has been mentioned
in other talks here,

00:33:03.640 --> 00:33:09.160
there are a lot of regulation
around sharing data.

00:33:09.160 --> 00:33:12.730
This is the simplified version
of the government's take

00:33:12.730 --> 00:33:15.251
on how we have to protect data.

00:33:15.251 --> 00:33:16.750
And what I want to
get across to you

00:33:16.750 --> 00:33:19.600
is that there's a
financial penalty here

00:33:19.600 --> 00:33:20.660
which is not cheap.

00:33:20.660 --> 00:33:22.850
It's $50,000 per violation.

00:33:22.850 --> 00:33:24.686
So if somebody
loses a spreadsheet

00:33:24.686 --> 00:33:27.310
with a bunch of patients' data,
it could run up in the millions

00:33:27.310 --> 00:33:28.259
very quickly.

00:33:28.259 --> 00:33:30.550
And we're talking about data
over hundreds of thousands

00:33:30.550 --> 00:33:31.480
of patients.

00:33:31.480 --> 00:33:36.340
But there's also what we used to
call the "above the fold" risk.

00:33:36.340 --> 00:33:37.720
Back when people
read newspapers,

00:33:37.720 --> 00:33:40.110
we had this concept that when
something was really bad,

00:33:40.110 --> 00:33:41.770
it would show up
on the front page

00:33:41.770 --> 00:33:43.930
and you would see it on
the stack of newspapers.

00:33:43.930 --> 00:33:45.970
The current version
of that is the wall

00:33:45.970 --> 00:33:48.940
of shame that's maintained by
the Health and Human Services

00:33:48.940 --> 00:33:51.040
which actually will
publish anybody that has

00:33:51.040 --> 00:33:52.990
a breach of over 500 patients.

00:33:52.990 --> 00:33:55.600
So you will end up on
here if you have a breach.

00:33:55.600 --> 00:33:58.682
And HHS now is
tweeting out whenever

00:33:58.682 --> 00:33:59.640
they post a new breach.

00:33:59.640 --> 00:34:02.500
And so you will get
tagged in their tweet.

00:34:02.500 --> 00:34:04.930
And you can now find
yourself on the wall of shame

00:34:04.930 --> 00:34:06.380
if you do this.

00:34:06.380 --> 00:34:08.650
So the risk is
actually very high,

00:34:08.650 --> 00:34:10.659
both from a financial
perspective as well as

00:34:10.659 --> 00:34:13.420
a reputational risk.

00:34:13.420 --> 00:34:16.540
HIPAA is the set of rules that
governs how we share data.

00:34:16.540 --> 00:34:20.800
It came out of the Recovery
and Reinvestment Act.

00:34:20.800 --> 00:34:22.480
And they've defined
18 identifiers

00:34:22.480 --> 00:34:25.719
of things you have to remove
to make it de-identified.

00:34:25.719 --> 00:34:28.360
And there's things you would
expect-- name, address, email

00:34:28.360 --> 00:34:30.030
address, and so forth.

00:34:30.030 --> 00:34:32.020
But just to make sure
that they got everything,

00:34:32.020 --> 00:34:33.850
they added this one
at the end here called

00:34:33.850 --> 00:34:36.587
"any other unique
identifying number."

00:34:36.587 --> 00:34:38.920
And I think the last talk
mentioned something about this

00:34:38.920 --> 00:34:41.889
too in the previous
session, that it

00:34:41.889 --> 00:34:43.989
can be any sort of
something that could

00:34:43.989 --> 00:34:45.790
be used to identify the person.

00:34:45.790 --> 00:34:47.290
And so you have to
really be careful

00:34:47.290 --> 00:34:49.123
as you share data that
if you just strip out

00:34:49.123 --> 00:34:51.239
these things that are
on this page here,

00:34:51.239 --> 00:34:52.750
that might not be enough.

00:34:52.750 --> 00:34:55.510
And we'll come back to that
toward the end of the talk.

00:34:55.510 --> 00:34:58.300
So one of the things to think
about in health care data

00:34:58.300 --> 00:35:00.520
is that it's
distributed everywhere.

00:35:00.520 --> 00:35:02.410
And that's actually
the dirty secret

00:35:02.410 --> 00:35:03.850
that people don't talk about.

00:35:03.850 --> 00:35:06.730
If you think about all the
places your health data are,

00:35:06.730 --> 00:35:07.796
they're everywhere.

00:35:07.796 --> 00:35:09.670
This was highlighted
during the election when

00:35:09.670 --> 00:35:12.340
there was these calls to
release the full medical record

00:35:12.340 --> 00:35:14.004
for the presidential candidates.

00:35:14.004 --> 00:35:15.670
Everybody wanted to
see the full record,

00:35:15.670 --> 00:35:18.070
and they were probably
imagining some stack of papers

00:35:18.070 --> 00:35:19.330
that was the full record.

00:35:19.330 --> 00:35:21.400
But as you've seen just
in the short talk so far,

00:35:21.400 --> 00:35:24.400
the full record includes
electronic health record data,

00:35:24.400 --> 00:35:27.010
even data that you're
finding on pagers, data

00:35:27.010 --> 00:35:28.790
that you're finding from pumps.

00:35:28.790 --> 00:35:30.199
It could be
scattered everywhere.

00:35:30.199 --> 00:35:31.990
And the interesting
quote from this article

00:35:31.990 --> 00:35:34.660
that the author wrote, he
wrote, "Her medical records

00:35:34.660 --> 00:35:37.100
are in bits and pieces, in
doctors' offices, filing

00:35:37.100 --> 00:35:39.230
cabinets, hospital
records departments,

00:35:39.230 --> 00:35:41.150
and hard-to-access computers.

00:35:41.150 --> 00:35:43.010
Just like yours, probably."

00:35:43.010 --> 00:35:46.130
And I would challenge anybody
to come up with all the spots

00:35:46.130 --> 00:35:47.780
that their medical
record exists,

00:35:47.780 --> 00:35:51.022
all the way down to the
grammar school office

00:35:51.022 --> 00:35:52.730
where your vaccination
record is probably

00:35:52.730 --> 00:35:54.510
sitting from years ago.

00:35:54.510 --> 00:35:56.940
So your data are
scattered everywhere.

00:35:56.940 --> 00:35:58.220
So what do we do about this?

00:35:58.220 --> 00:36:01.520
We want to get the data into a
place where we can analyze it,

00:36:01.520 --> 00:36:03.860
and we can do this
safely and with good

00:36:03.860 --> 00:36:04.910
protection of privacy.

00:36:04.910 --> 00:36:07.070
We need to fill up a
warehouse with data.

00:36:07.070 --> 00:36:10.020
So at the University of Chicago,
the way we've approached this

00:36:10.020 --> 00:36:11.300
is to--

00:36:11.300 --> 00:36:13.700
we call it getting our
hands dirty with the data.

00:36:13.700 --> 00:36:16.220
We have a group that I run
called the Center for Research

00:36:16.220 --> 00:36:17.009
Informatics.

00:36:17.009 --> 00:36:18.800
The guys that work for
me, I call them data

00:36:18.800 --> 00:36:20.810
wranglers because
they get in there,

00:36:20.810 --> 00:36:23.570
they learn about the
data, they model the data,

00:36:23.570 --> 00:36:25.250
they make sure that
we're understanding

00:36:25.250 --> 00:36:28.820
what's coming through as we take
data from the hospital systems.

00:36:28.820 --> 00:36:32.150
And we built what we think is
a very well organized, clean

00:36:32.150 --> 00:36:34.490
warehouse where when
you want to request data

00:36:34.490 --> 00:36:36.920
on a particular disease, we
can get at the data in a way

00:36:36.920 --> 00:36:39.860
that you have assurances
about the quality of the data

00:36:39.860 --> 00:36:40.730
you're getting.

00:36:40.730 --> 00:36:44.384
And I think one of the exciting
things about collaborating,

00:36:44.384 --> 00:36:46.550
working with Scott and
working with the Google team,

00:36:46.550 --> 00:36:50.390
is that we have a mutual
expectation about the quality

00:36:50.390 --> 00:36:52.190
of the data and the
quality of the analysis

00:36:52.190 --> 00:36:53.481
that's going to come out of it.

00:36:56.900 --> 00:36:58.834
MICHAEL HOWELL:
So the other piece

00:36:58.834 --> 00:37:00.250
to think about
with health care is

00:37:00.250 --> 00:37:03.980
that it may be organized
differently than you think.

00:37:03.980 --> 00:37:09.520
And the one study that I'll show
you today that someone else did

00:37:09.520 --> 00:37:13.129
is how much R&amp;D is done in the
health care delivery system.

00:37:13.129 --> 00:37:14.920
When we think about
the health care system,

00:37:14.920 --> 00:37:18.820
we think about biotech and
pharma and other areas.

00:37:18.820 --> 00:37:21.160
But, in fact, the health
care delivery system

00:37:21.160 --> 00:37:29.770
spends less than 1% of revenues
on R&amp;D. The food and beverage

00:37:29.770 --> 00:37:32.620
industry spends
fivefold more on R&amp;D

00:37:32.620 --> 00:37:36.370
than the health care
delivery system does today.

00:37:36.370 --> 00:37:40.340
And that is, in some ways,
one of the crippling problems

00:37:40.340 --> 00:37:42.340
of modern American health
care is that we're not

00:37:42.340 --> 00:37:43.970
organized in that way.

00:37:43.970 --> 00:37:46.199
And so one of the things
that we've attempted to do--

00:37:46.199 --> 00:37:48.490
and we'll show you how this
plays out in a little bit--

00:37:48.490 --> 00:37:52.750
is reorganize in a way where
the folks doing operations

00:37:52.750 --> 00:37:54.970
in health care and the
folks doing research

00:37:54.970 --> 00:37:56.620
don't live in different places.

00:37:56.620 --> 00:37:58.480
We call that our
Center for Healthcare

00:37:58.480 --> 00:38:00.640
Delivery Science and Innovation.

00:38:00.640 --> 00:38:02.140
And it's one of the
things that lets

00:38:02.140 --> 00:38:05.410
us work on really interesting
collaborations that impact

00:38:05.410 --> 00:38:10.230
patients today, not 40
years in the future.

00:38:10.230 --> 00:38:13.110
I am going to return
and try and give

00:38:13.110 --> 00:38:17.040
an example of this in
action because why do we

00:38:17.040 --> 00:38:17.940
care about data?

00:38:17.940 --> 00:38:21.790
We do care about-- data
helps us get more efficient.

00:38:21.790 --> 00:38:23.580
It helps us make margin.

00:38:23.580 --> 00:38:25.890
It helps us reduce waste.

00:38:25.890 --> 00:38:28.810
But it also impacts real
people in the real world.

00:38:28.810 --> 00:38:31.319
And we'll give you two examples
of how we've done that.

00:38:31.319 --> 00:38:33.360
So we said, what's the
difference between a heart

00:38:33.360 --> 00:38:34.950
attack and a cardiac arrest?

00:38:34.950 --> 00:38:37.500
95% of people with a
heart attack survive.

00:38:37.500 --> 00:38:40.305
Cardiac arrest is the
recognition that you are dead.

00:38:40.305 --> 00:38:46.140
And CPR doesn't work 80%
of the time, unlike on TV.

00:38:46.140 --> 00:38:48.090
And so the University
of Chicago has

00:38:48.090 --> 00:38:50.520
a long history of
excellence in cardiac arrest

00:38:50.520 --> 00:38:53.130
research starting more
than a decade ago,

00:38:53.130 --> 00:38:56.730
and with a team led by Matt
Churpek and Dana Edelson,

00:38:56.730 --> 00:38:59.790
has been doing
predictive work in how

00:38:59.790 --> 00:39:02.250
is it you would figure out who
would have a cardiac arrest

00:39:02.250 --> 00:39:07.320
or develop critical illness
before that happens.

00:39:07.320 --> 00:39:10.530
In the end, they did
a five-hospital study

00:39:10.530 --> 00:39:12.490
with more than a quarter
million patients.

00:39:12.490 --> 00:39:15.360
And I'm a predictive
modeler at heart.

00:39:15.360 --> 00:39:18.180
This thing warms--
it warms my heart.

00:39:18.180 --> 00:39:20.550
It's a 27-dimensional model.

00:39:20.550 --> 00:39:23.220
It's got spline terms,
the whole nine yards.

00:39:23.220 --> 00:39:25.620
And if you like
areas under the curve

00:39:25.620 --> 00:39:30.000
as a way of assessing
how useful a model is,

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:32.310
this is today the
best predictive model

00:39:32.310 --> 00:39:37.990
for cardiac arrest on the planet
developed in our patients.

00:39:37.990 --> 00:39:40.710
But at the end of this, what
do these researchers have?

00:39:40.710 --> 00:39:45.060
They have a paper published
in a journal affecting

00:39:45.060 --> 00:39:46.230
no patient anywhere.

00:39:46.230 --> 00:39:48.350
And so we've been
able to take this.

00:39:48.350 --> 00:39:51.000
We have an enterprise
services bus architecture

00:39:51.000 --> 00:39:54.030
at the University of
Chicago, turned this live,

00:39:54.030 --> 00:39:57.590
and see over and above our
usual methods for responding.

00:39:57.590 --> 00:40:00.720
We've seen a 20% reduction
in cardiac arrest

00:40:00.720 --> 00:40:04.980
in about a year and a half span
after implementation of this.

00:40:04.980 --> 00:40:08.880
That's a pretty clever thing
that we've been able to do,

00:40:08.880 --> 00:40:12.870
and it's gotten some news
about how we've taken things

00:40:12.870 --> 00:40:15.150
from the data warehouse,
from the Center for Research

00:40:15.150 --> 00:40:19.320
Informatics, turned it into
something in the real world.

00:40:19.320 --> 00:40:21.090
But there's another
interesting question

00:40:21.090 --> 00:40:22.650
that now we can attack.

00:40:22.650 --> 00:40:25.050
And it's actually
the question of,

00:40:25.050 --> 00:40:26.860
can critical illness
be contagious?

00:40:26.860 --> 00:40:29.250
So imagine that
you're hospitalized.

00:40:29.250 --> 00:40:32.580
And it's a 10-bed unit, and
you're in the third bed,

00:40:32.580 --> 00:40:34.950
or your mom's in the
third bed, or your brother

00:40:34.950 --> 00:40:36.780
or your daughter.

00:40:36.780 --> 00:40:39.360
And what happens if
the person in bed six

00:40:39.360 --> 00:40:43.210
gets sick and needs the ICU
or has a cardiac arrest?

00:40:43.210 --> 00:40:44.400
What happens to you?

00:40:44.400 --> 00:40:46.887
Is the risk contagious?

00:40:46.887 --> 00:40:48.720
And so if you thought
for a minute about how

00:40:48.720 --> 00:40:51.120
you would study that, it's
actually a really difficult

00:40:51.120 --> 00:40:52.050
problem to sort out.

00:40:52.050 --> 00:40:55.200
And so we took about
90,000 patients' data,

00:40:55.200 --> 00:40:57.780
time-ordered them, broke
them down into time blocks,

00:40:57.780 --> 00:41:01.080
ran the 27-dimensional
multivariable model, sorted

00:41:01.080 --> 00:41:03.750
out a zillion other
factors, and we

00:41:03.750 --> 00:41:06.630
we're able to answer the
question of this, that,

00:41:06.630 --> 00:41:11.700
in fact, yeah, if that
person has [? crumps ?]

00:41:11.700 --> 00:41:14.130
and goes to the ICU,
for the next six hours,

00:41:14.130 --> 00:41:16.770
your risk of developing
critical illness

00:41:16.770 --> 00:41:21.240
goes up by 18% after
extremely careful control

00:41:21.240 --> 00:41:23.070
for how sick you are.

00:41:23.070 --> 00:41:26.100
And if two people
have that happen,

00:41:26.100 --> 00:41:29.790
then the risk goes up by 54%.

00:41:29.790 --> 00:41:32.400
And so now we're
attempting to turn

00:41:32.400 --> 00:41:34.230
that live into,
how do we actually

00:41:34.230 --> 00:41:37.080
deal with and adjust with that?

00:41:37.080 --> 00:41:38.790
But these are examples
of the reasons

00:41:38.790 --> 00:41:42.270
that we care so much
about the quality of data,

00:41:42.270 --> 00:41:44.520
how we get the data
sorted, and then

00:41:44.520 --> 00:41:47.340
how we begin to
be able to use it.

00:41:47.340 --> 00:41:50.391
But privacy protection is
critical to making discoveries

00:41:50.391 --> 00:41:50.890
like this.

00:41:50.890 --> 00:41:52.950
We can't do it without it.

00:41:52.950 --> 00:41:56.594
And it's really hard
to do privacy well

00:41:56.594 --> 00:41:58.260
while making discoveries
that still save

00:41:58.260 --> 00:42:02.060
lives if you're moving
outside your own institution.

00:42:02.060 --> 00:42:04.310
And so we've said health
care data may be a little bit

00:42:04.310 --> 00:42:04.810
different.

00:42:04.810 --> 00:42:07.340
Privacy in health care may
be a little bit different.

00:42:07.340 --> 00:42:10.640
Health care may not have R&amp;D
as you would recognize it,

00:42:10.640 --> 00:42:14.630
and that privacy protection
is critical to discovery.

00:42:14.630 --> 00:42:17.089
And so we are really
excited about the changes

00:42:17.089 --> 00:42:19.130
that have been happening
over the past few years.

00:42:19.130 --> 00:42:22.670
We are really good on our
own at using discrete data

00:42:22.670 --> 00:42:24.440
for predicting things.

00:42:24.440 --> 00:42:25.910
We've been doing
that for a while.

00:42:25.910 --> 00:42:29.280
It's not easy, but we've been
sorting out how to do it.

00:42:29.280 --> 00:42:32.210
But today, we're beginning
to be able to think about how

00:42:32.210 --> 00:42:34.760
we use the whole electronic
health record, including

00:42:34.760 --> 00:42:38.120
the notes that doctors and
nurses and others write.

00:42:38.120 --> 00:42:39.890
And one of the
problems is if you're

00:42:39.890 --> 00:42:42.540
trained like I am,
as an epidemiologist,

00:42:42.540 --> 00:42:44.780
as a health care
researcher, once you

00:42:44.780 --> 00:42:50.010
get to 400, 500, 600 variables,
my models don't work.

00:42:50.010 --> 00:42:52.700
But if we start using
free-text notes,

00:42:52.700 --> 00:42:56.285
there are really new
challenges that are hard.

00:42:56.285 --> 00:42:58.160
SAMUEL VOLCHENBOUM: OK,
so hang in there now.

00:42:58.160 --> 00:42:59.743
I'm going to take
you through a couple

00:42:59.743 --> 00:43:01.940
examples of some of
the challenges of using

00:43:01.940 --> 00:43:02.810
free-text notes.

00:43:02.810 --> 00:43:04.460
And I think they're
going to highlight

00:43:04.460 --> 00:43:07.220
what we're so excited about
when it comes to collaborating

00:43:07.220 --> 00:43:09.440
with Google on trying
to think about how

00:43:09.440 --> 00:43:12.290
to do de-identification
of free-text notes

00:43:12.290 --> 00:43:16.550
and how to do this analysis
over large sets of patient data.

00:43:16.550 --> 00:43:20.510
This is an example of a
couple of notes on a patient.

00:43:20.510 --> 00:43:23.810
The note says Mr. Smith is a
65-year-old man with weight

00:43:23.810 --> 00:43:26.060
loss admitted on August 1.

00:43:26.060 --> 00:43:28.760
Mr. Smith lives with
his sister, Jane.

00:43:28.760 --> 00:43:31.790
And there's a family history
that Jane had tuberculosis

00:43:31.790 --> 00:43:33.840
diagnosed in February.

00:43:33.840 --> 00:43:35.510
So if you're seeing
this guy, he's

00:43:35.510 --> 00:43:37.431
got this weird,
unexplained weight loss

00:43:37.431 --> 00:43:39.430
and he's living with
somebody with tuberculosis,

00:43:39.430 --> 00:43:42.140
you immediately start thinking,
could there be a connection?

00:43:42.140 --> 00:43:44.570
Well, if we tried to
de-identify this information

00:43:44.570 --> 00:43:46.250
using standard
redaction, we'll take out

00:43:46.250 --> 00:43:48.560
the names and the exact dates.

00:43:48.560 --> 00:43:51.620
And we're basically
left with blank

00:43:51.620 --> 00:43:53.840
is a 65-year-old man
with weight loss admitted

00:43:53.840 --> 00:43:55.490
sometime last year.

00:43:55.490 --> 00:43:57.140
Blank lives with
his sister, blank.

00:43:57.140 --> 00:44:00.180
And blank had tuberculosis
diagnosed sometime.

00:44:00.180 --> 00:44:01.140
Useless, right?

00:44:01.140 --> 00:44:02.870
Like, that doesn't
tell us anything.

00:44:02.870 --> 00:44:04.786
Well, we can do a little
bit smarter than that

00:44:04.786 --> 00:44:08.810
and we can actually
instead take the note,

00:44:08.810 --> 00:44:10.730
and we can take out
the name, but we

00:44:10.730 --> 00:44:12.480
can preserve the admit date.

00:44:12.480 --> 00:44:15.890
So we can say that so-and-so
is a 65-year-old man admitted

00:44:15.890 --> 00:44:18.120
with weight loss on some date.

00:44:18.120 --> 00:44:19.670
And now we can use
the colors to say,

00:44:19.670 --> 00:44:21.920
well, so-and-so lives with
his sister, another person,

00:44:21.920 --> 00:44:24.410
and that other person
had tuberculosis,

00:44:24.410 --> 00:44:26.160
and there's a
relationship there.

00:44:26.160 --> 00:44:27.920
So this removes the
HIPAA identifiers

00:44:27.920 --> 00:44:29.990
and it satisfies the
de-identification,

00:44:29.990 --> 00:44:31.880
but still, the computer's
not telling us,

00:44:31.880 --> 00:44:33.800
which is painfully
obvious to everybody who's

00:44:33.800 --> 00:44:38.199
looking at this is,
yeah, something else

00:44:38.199 --> 00:44:39.740
could be going on,
but I really think

00:44:39.740 --> 00:44:41.240
that you should
think about he might

00:44:41.240 --> 00:44:42.740
have got TB from his sister.

00:44:42.740 --> 00:44:45.590
So this is an example of a
relatively simple heuristic

00:44:45.590 --> 00:44:47.660
for a human to come up
with, but a difficult text

00:44:47.660 --> 00:44:50.150
problem for a computer
to solve if you

00:44:50.150 --> 00:44:52.890
want to maintain privacy.

00:44:52.890 --> 00:44:55.451
So why is de-identification
in health care a hard problem?

00:44:55.451 --> 00:44:56.950
Well, it's hard for
several reasons.

00:44:56.950 --> 00:45:00.240
One is that we're trained to
think that we need to connect

00:45:00.240 --> 00:45:01.500
the data with the outcomes.

00:45:01.500 --> 00:45:04.140
And for discrete data, that
can be relatively easy.

00:45:04.140 --> 00:45:06.150
But with free text,
it can be much more

00:45:06.150 --> 00:45:08.970
difficult to know what the data
are, the discrete data that's

00:45:08.970 --> 00:45:11.920
in the text, and what
the outcomes are.

00:45:11.920 --> 00:45:13.590
We must remove the
HIPAA identifiers,

00:45:13.590 --> 00:45:15.630
including the dates.

00:45:15.630 --> 00:45:18.330
And in practice, when we do this
with discrete data, like blood

00:45:18.330 --> 00:45:20.250
pressure and heart rate,
it's easy to remove

00:45:20.250 --> 00:45:21.540
any of the HIPAA identifiers.

00:45:21.540 --> 00:45:24.450
But as we move into free text,
it becomes very difficult

00:45:24.450 --> 00:45:27.030
and you need a high-performance
environment to do this.

00:45:27.030 --> 00:45:29.749
When we were first thinking
about doing this collaboration,

00:45:29.749 --> 00:45:31.290
we thought, how long
would it take us

00:45:31.290 --> 00:45:33.750
to de-identify our 20
million text notes?

00:45:33.750 --> 00:45:36.030
And the first feedback
I got from my team,

00:45:36.030 --> 00:45:38.160
that even on our
high-performance environment,

00:45:38.160 --> 00:45:41.460
it would take us
six months to do it.

00:45:41.460 --> 00:45:44.370
So I have a relatively complex
example to take us on out,

00:45:44.370 --> 00:45:45.990
but I think it's
important and try

00:45:45.990 --> 00:45:47.460
to hang in there
with me because I

00:45:47.460 --> 00:45:51.404
think this is very illustrative
of the opportunity here.

00:45:51.404 --> 00:45:52.320
So here's Frank Jones.

00:45:52.320 --> 00:45:55.129
He's an 85-year-old man
here for his annual checkup.

00:45:55.129 --> 00:45:56.670
He's been doing well
but recently was

00:45:56.670 --> 00:45:59.340
forced to move from his
home in Batavia, Illinois

00:45:59.340 --> 00:46:01.800
to Elgin due to
insufficient funds.

00:46:01.800 --> 00:46:03.660
His eldest son,
Craig, was recently

00:46:03.660 --> 00:46:05.730
killed in an
automobile accident.

00:46:05.730 --> 00:46:09.120
Mr. Jones' remaining seven
children all live in Illinois.

00:46:09.120 --> 00:46:11.460
Mr. Jones' orthopedic
surgeon, Dr. Reeves,

00:46:11.460 --> 00:46:13.950
has been concerned about his
leg following his amputation

00:46:13.950 --> 00:46:14.970
last year for diabetes.

00:46:14.970 --> 00:46:18.060
But overall, he reports
no new medical issues.

00:46:18.060 --> 00:46:20.160
OK, so there's a lot
of identifiers there.

00:46:20.160 --> 00:46:23.670
So let's just redact out
the identifiers, his name.

00:46:23.670 --> 00:46:28.110
And you're left with this note
that takes out all the names.

00:46:28.110 --> 00:46:30.840
And there aren't any
dates in here, right?

00:46:30.840 --> 00:46:33.700
And there aren't any addresses,
and there's no full zip code.

00:46:33.700 --> 00:46:36.690
So this note, by all
accounts and purposes,

00:46:36.690 --> 00:46:38.940
would look to be
de-identified, right?

00:46:38.940 --> 00:46:41.850
But if you think back to
the k-anonymity lecture

00:46:41.850 --> 00:46:43.994
we had before from
Scott, there's

00:46:43.994 --> 00:46:45.660
something wrong with
this note because I

00:46:45.660 --> 00:46:48.280
would argue that it's not been
de-identified appropriately.

00:46:48.280 --> 00:46:51.000
So what identifiers
remain in this note?

00:46:51.000 --> 00:46:53.070
Well, I would argue that
being an 85-year-old man

00:46:53.070 --> 00:46:58.470
in a small town in Illinois
is not a very anonymous thing.

00:46:58.470 --> 00:47:01.350
The fact that his son was killed
in a car accident; the fact

00:47:01.350 --> 00:47:07.380
that he is an amputee; the fact
that he has eight children,

00:47:07.380 --> 00:47:08.670
now seven children--

00:47:08.670 --> 00:47:10.170
I would say that
all of those things

00:47:10.170 --> 00:47:13.800
reduce this person's
k-anonymity number very low

00:47:13.800 --> 00:47:18.530
so that this note is
actually quite identifiable.

00:47:18.530 --> 00:47:21.780
So if we're left with looking
at all these identifiers,

00:47:21.780 --> 00:47:23.580
why don't we just take them out?

00:47:23.580 --> 00:47:25.750
All right, so
here's the note now.

00:47:25.750 --> 00:47:28.681
I would argue now that this
note is pretty much useless.

00:47:28.681 --> 00:47:30.430
We've taken out all
the meaning, and we're

00:47:30.430 --> 00:47:32.263
left with a bunch of
Xs in here, and there's

00:47:32.263 --> 00:47:33.430
no meaning in the note.

00:47:33.430 --> 00:47:35.800
I think this is the part
where it gets really cool.

00:47:35.800 --> 00:47:38.740
So wouldn't it be
cool, really, if we

00:47:38.740 --> 00:47:40.840
used the power of
all the information

00:47:40.840 --> 00:47:43.930
that a company like Google
has at its fingertips to,

00:47:43.930 --> 00:47:47.140
in real time, think about,
what is the age distribution

00:47:47.140 --> 00:47:48.580
in small town Illinois?

00:47:48.580 --> 00:47:50.800
Is it safe to have an
85-year-old called out here?

00:47:50.800 --> 00:47:52.240
Do we have to change that?

00:47:52.240 --> 00:47:55.750
What about the fact that he
had a son killed in an accident

00:47:55.750 --> 00:47:57.454
or that he's an amputee?

00:47:57.454 --> 00:47:59.620
Google can check to see,
was that in a public record

00:47:59.620 --> 00:48:00.120
somewhere?

00:48:00.120 --> 00:48:02.725
Was there a newspaper
article about it?

00:48:02.725 --> 00:48:05.890
Is there a publicly available
obituary that says this?

00:48:05.890 --> 00:48:08.200
What about the fact that
he has a huge family?

00:48:08.200 --> 00:48:10.660
Is that a common thing
in central Illinois?

00:48:10.660 --> 00:48:12.160
Do we need to
change that as well?

00:48:12.160 --> 00:48:14.500
And in real time, you could
take this note, actually,

00:48:14.500 --> 00:48:18.340
and turn it into something that
does some of this obscuring

00:48:18.340 --> 00:48:19.330
by changing the names.

00:48:19.330 --> 00:48:20.860
Maybe you could change
the age a little bit.

00:48:20.860 --> 00:48:22.359
But if you changed
the age, you want

00:48:22.359 --> 00:48:25.207
to do it in a way that maintains
the information in the note.

00:48:25.207 --> 00:48:27.040
There's an amazing array
of different things

00:48:27.040 --> 00:48:28.750
that we could
consider if you think

00:48:28.750 --> 00:48:31.030
about the power of all the
other reference data that

00:48:31.030 --> 00:48:33.170
lives underneath the hood.

00:48:33.170 --> 00:48:36.910
So de-identification is a
huge problem for research

00:48:36.910 --> 00:48:40.390
because we need so much data
to study rare phenomenon,

00:48:40.390 --> 00:48:42.490
like the kind that Michael
talked about, looking

00:48:42.490 --> 00:48:44.860
for patients going to the ICU.

00:48:44.860 --> 00:48:48.010
We needed 90,000 patients
to pick up even the smallest

00:48:48.010 --> 00:48:49.060
amount of signal.

00:48:49.060 --> 00:48:51.520
So, ideally, you would do
this for many hospitals,

00:48:51.520 --> 00:48:54.080
and we would sort of require
you to collaborate and to share

00:48:54.080 --> 00:48:54.580
data.

00:48:54.580 --> 00:48:56.080
And to do that,
you're going to have

00:48:56.080 --> 00:48:58.540
to standardize the data
while de-identifying it

00:48:58.540 --> 00:49:00.670
and satisfying all the
different privacy boards

00:49:00.670 --> 00:49:03.070
that you're not going to do
anything that compromises

00:49:03.070 --> 00:49:04.440
the patients' privacy.

00:49:04.440 --> 00:49:06.190
So that's why we're
here today, and that's

00:49:06.190 --> 00:49:07.780
why we're so excited.

00:49:07.780 --> 00:49:09.970
We're really looking forward
to this collaboration

00:49:09.970 --> 00:49:13.150
because we're going to
understand how to use and study

00:49:13.150 --> 00:49:16.134
sensitive data at large scale.

00:49:16.134 --> 00:49:17.800
There's a lot we have
to do, and there's

00:49:17.800 --> 00:49:19.842
a lot we're going to have
to do to get this done.

00:49:19.842 --> 00:49:21.216
We're going to
have to figure out

00:49:21.216 --> 00:49:23.380
how this de-identification
process makes research

00:49:23.380 --> 00:49:26.440
more difficult. What is
the spectrum of, as you

00:49:26.440 --> 00:49:27.520
de-identify things?

00:49:27.520 --> 00:49:30.580
Does it make research
harder to do?

00:49:30.580 --> 00:49:31.792
When is HIPAA not enough?

00:49:31.792 --> 00:49:33.250
I showed you that
note before and I

00:49:33.250 --> 00:49:35.440
argued that we removed
all the HIPAA identifiers,

00:49:35.440 --> 00:49:38.080
but you would say HIPAA
is not enough there.

00:49:38.080 --> 00:49:41.020
We need to consider the other
ways you can identify people.

00:49:41.020 --> 00:49:43.480
So can these new
approaches maintain privacy

00:49:43.480 --> 00:49:47.720
while preserving the research
value of the information?

00:49:47.720 --> 00:49:49.810
And finally, what tools
can privacy boards

00:49:49.810 --> 00:49:51.310
use to protect patients?

00:49:51.310 --> 00:49:54.940
Right now, whenever our
IRB has a tough question,

00:49:54.940 --> 00:49:57.670
they turn to Michael and me
and say, is this good enough?

00:49:57.670 --> 00:50:00.040
And I would think there's
a better set of tools

00:50:00.040 --> 00:50:01.570
that privacy boards
can start to use

00:50:01.570 --> 00:50:04.510
to standardize how we
think about privacy

00:50:04.510 --> 00:50:06.160
protection for patients.

00:50:06.160 --> 00:50:09.310
So we really think that better
de-identification and better

00:50:09.310 --> 00:50:11.410
analytics tools is going
to speed up health care

00:50:11.410 --> 00:50:13.510
discoveries and is
really going to help

00:50:13.510 --> 00:50:16.900
improve patient outcomes.

00:50:16.900 --> 00:50:19.370
by letting us study these
enormous large sets of data.

00:50:19.370 --> 00:50:20.919
So that's why
we're very excited.

00:50:20.919 --> 00:50:23.460
Here's some of the groups that
have helped make this possible

00:50:23.460 --> 00:50:24.590
at University of Chicago.

00:50:24.590 --> 00:50:26.500
And if we have time, hopefully,
for Scott and Michael

00:50:26.500 --> 00:50:28.370
and myself, we can
answer some questions.

00:50:28.370 --> 00:50:29.050
Thank you.

00:50:29.050 --> 00:50:33.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

